Root cause:
The bug is caused by the premature freeing of the `sr` pointer before it is dereferenced to read the error code. This leads to a use-after-free condition, where the code attempts to access memory that has already been released, potentially resulting in undefined behavior or crashes.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
softreq_destroy(sr);
err = READ_ONCE(*sr->resp.orh) & 0xff;
```
Reason:
The `sr` pointer is freed by the `softreq_destroy(sr)` call before it is dereferenced to read the error code with `READ_ONCE(*sr->resp.orh)`. This results in a use-after-free condition, where the code attempts to access memory that has already been released.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
err = READ_ONCE(*sr->resp.orh) & 0xff;
softreq_destroy(sr);
```
Reason:
By moving the `softreq_destroy(sr)` call after the dereference of `sr` to read the error code, the code ensures that `sr` is still valid when accessed. This prevents the use-after-free condition and ensures that the error code can be read safely before the memory is released.


